  "locales": {
    "en": {
      "title": "Possessive pronouns",
      "summary": "A possessive pronoun stands alone in place of a noun and its owner.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What it is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "A possessive pronoun shows who owns something, and it replaces the whole noun phrase: Whose is this? It is mine. English has mine, yours, his, hers, ours and theirs (and its, which is rare)."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "That book is **mine**.",
                "Is this pen **yours**?",
                "The decision is **theirs**."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Possessive pronouns and possessive adjectives",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Do not confuse the pronoun with the adjective. A possessive adjective (my, your, his, her, its, our, their) stands before a noun. A possessive pronoun stands alone, with no noun after it."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Adjective + noun</th><th>Pronoun alone</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>my book</strong></td><td><strong>mine</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>your coat</strong></td><td><strong>yours</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>his house</strong></td><td><strong>his</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>her bag</strong></td><td><strong>hers</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>our car</strong></td><td><strong>ours</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>their idea</strong></td><td><strong>theirs</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "How to use them",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Use a possessive pronoun to avoid repeating a noun, and in the pattern a friend of mine, which means one of my friends. The pronoun has no apostrophe: it is yours, not your's."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>avoiding repetition</td><td><strong>My car is blue; hers is red.</strong></td></tr><tr><td>a friend of mine</td><td><strong>He is a friend of mine.</strong></td></tr><tr><td>after be</td><td><strong>The idea was entirely hers.</strong></td></tr><tr><td>in comparisons</td><td><strong>Our results are better than theirs.</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Common mistakes",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Wrong: **This is mine book.** Right: **This is my book.**",
                "Wrong: **a friend of me** Right: **a friend of mine**",
                "Wrong: **The dog hurt it's leg.** Right: **its leg** (it's means it is)",
                "Wrong: **Is it your's?** Right: **Is it yours?**"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
